# Dile — keyboard access for whoever is logged in at this machine.
#
# Dile reads key presses from the kernel's input devices instead of from a window system.
# That is what makes one trigger behave the same under Wayland, under X11 and on a bare
# console, and it is the only way to get hold-to-talk on a right Ctrl at all: a Wayland
# compositor will not hand a lone modifier to an application, and the desktop shortcut
# registries it offers instead have no release event, which turns hold-to-talk into toggle.
#
# Two nodes, one permission:
#
#   /dev/input/event*   reading a press
#   /dev/uinput         swallowing one. A blocked key means the keyboard is grabbed and
#                       everything that is not the trigger is re-injected through a clone,
#                       and the clone is a uinput device. Dile asks for this even with the
#                       shipped lone-modifier trigger, which blocks nothing, because the
#                       review card adds Enter, Esc and Ctrl+C to the same set while it is
#                       on screen — an Enter that transfers the text and also lands in the
#                       document behind it is the bug this prevents.
#
# TAG+="uaccess" rather than a group. systemd-logind hands the device to the user who is
# currently logged in at the seat, as an ACL, and takes it back when they log out. The
# alternative that is usually suggested — usermod -aG input $USER — grants every process
# that account ever starts, an SSH session from another continent included, the ability to
# read every keystroke on this machine, permanently. This rule grants the same capability to
# the person sitting in front of it, for as long as they are sitting in front of it.
#
# **Be clear about what that is.** Any program running as that user can then read the
# keyboard, whatever window has the focus. Wayland closes that door on purpose and this rule
# opens it again for the local seat. It is the same trade every push-to-talk tool on Linux
# makes; Dile's part of the bargain is that the audio and the text never leave the machine.
#
# The number matters: uaccess tags are collected by 73-seat-late.rules, so a rule that
# applies one has to be read before it. Anything below 73 works; 70 leaves room.
#
# **The .deb and the .rpm install this file**, at /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/, and their
# post-install script reloads udev so that it takes effect without a logout — see
# packaging/linux/post-install.sh. That is a decision rather than a default: a package that
# asked for the permission at first launch instead would be asking in a dialog, from a tray
# application, about a file only root can write, and the honest place to say what a package
# grants is the package.
#
# From a source build, by hand, which is the same two commands the package runs:
#
#   sudo install -m 0644 packaging/linux/70-dile-input.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger
#
# It takes effect immediately and needs no logout.

SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", TAG+="uaccess"
KERNEL=="uinput", TAG+="uaccess"
